  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Drought and flood-resistant seeds can increase the resilience of farmers to climate change. Australia&#x2019;s Market Development Facility (MDF) has worked in partnership with the private sector (East-West Seeds and KK Hardware) to introduce drought and flood resistant seeds into Fiji. Commercial results from the introduction are promising:  Several varieties survived heatwaves and droughts, producing crops of vegetables and providing more stable income for farmers. The seeds required less water, fertiliser and chemical inputs, contributing to soil health. Farmers reported increased yields and that they have been able to plant during the low season and capitalise on higher prices. With 496 farming households benefitting in Fiji, MDF is now working with the private sector to expand the availability of high-quality seeds in Pacific island countries and Timor-Leste, with a company distributing the seeds in Samoa.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Fiji, MDFworked with Ram Sami and Baywater Engineering to introduce domestically produced organic fertiliser made from industrial food waste (such as chicken manure and distillery waste). These locally produced organic solutions improve soil health, increase agricultural production and a provide a cost-effective alternative to imported chemical fertilisers that are produced and transported using fossil fuels.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In PNG the Market Development Facility (MDF) partnered with local coffee exporter, Kosem Coffee, to establish a &#x2018;revolving fund&#x2019; to increase purchase of high grade coffee through its supply chain. The credit system provides Kosem with cash to buy more coffee from smallholders during harvest, and is replenished when Kosem receives payment from exporters. It has allowed Kosem to significantly increase sourcing from remote communities (additional 24,000 kg coffee in 2022 cf 2021) for export, improving market access for more farmers. It also provides higher and more stable income for small holder farmers especially in Jiwaka and Western Highlands provinces.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">MDF supports renewable energy firms to better assess market demand, analyse the costs and benefits of adopting renewable technologies, and communicate these to their customers. This includes conducting market assessment of rooftop solar in Tonga, Samoa and Vanuatu, and piloting electric mini vans for the tourism industry in Fiji.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sri Lanka&#x2019;s specialty coffee sector has seen significant growth in recent years, driven by MDF&#x2019;s strategic innovations and partnerships with businesses.
&#x200d;
MDF has worked with the private sector to support improvements in quality across the coffee value chain, such as expanded planting of coffee by tea estates and smallholders, nursery expansion, selective picking of coffee cherries, climate-resilient drying houses, centralised processing, and promoting Sri Lankan coffee domestically and internationally. This support has boosted the sector and inspired replication across regions of Sri Lanka.
&#x200d;
MDF&#x2019;s work has led to increased incomes of 2,407 individuals across the sector. Since 2017, USD 566,350 (56% private sector) has been invested in specialty coffee, increasing processing capacity 27-fold since 2021. This has generated USD 1.2 million in revenue and USD 123,252 in added farmer income.
&#x200d;
Importantly, women now represent over 52% of the sector&#x2019;s beneficiaries, highlighting inclusive growth.
&#x200d;
MDF continues to invest in developing Sri Lankan coffee, including through exploring new technology in terms of quality control and traceability and co-development of the National Coffee Roadmap with the Department of Export Agriculture.
&#x200d;
With rising government commitment and expanding market interest, Sri Lanka&#x2019;s specialty coffee is poised for a vibrant future.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic empowerment</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Market Development Facility (MDF) and the Tongan Ministry of Trade &amp; Economic Development (MTED) collaborated to develop and operate a Tonga Business Accelerator. This was informed by MDF&#x2019;s prior work developing the Fiji Enterprise Engine. In 2024, MDF helped MTED train additional people to become business coaches to facilitate future business accelerator programs. 
&#x200d;
The Ministry of Trade &amp; Economic Development and its trained business coaches then independently organised and ran three accelerator programs during 2024, involving 60 Tongan micro, small and medium businesses. Of the 60 participants, 34 (57 per cent) were female. These women own and operate businesses in several areas including catering, baking and food; nurseries; handicrafts; financial services; construction, engineering; and tailoring.
&#x200d;
MTED also developed a small grants scheme in connection with the Business Accelerator. Of the 34 female participants in 2024, 15 received grants of TOP 5,000 (AUD 3,200), some using MDF support. Most of the entrepreneurs put this funding towards equipment and materials to support business expansion. Ms. Telusila Moala, a participant in the accelerator training, said: &#x201c;After the training, I can understand how to operate a business better. Thank you for the opportunity, and look forward for more training in the future.&#x201d;</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The most frequent challenge raised by Samoan farmers is the impact of feral pigs. Field research by the Market Development Facility (MDF) revealed that 74% of farmers suffered feral pig damage to crops and pasture. Feral pigs damage many crops, especially taro, the staple food of Samoa, contributing to high prices and food insecurity. Some farmers are hunting or moving plantations, whilst others are giving up planting crops altogether.
&#x200d;
MDF is working with the Scientific Research Organisation of Samoa (SROS) and Samoa&#x2019;s Ministry of Agriculture and Fisheries (MAF) to better understand the extent and nature of the feral pig problem, and trial management measures.  In 2024, MDF helped the Samoan Government trial a net-based trap. Based on this trial, the Government purchased one hundred additional feral pig traps, which they are rolling out to communities across Samoa.  
&#x200d;
Through a visit to Queensland, the Samoan Government learned about Australia&#x2019;s National Feral Pig Action Plan, and the experience of the Junjuwarra Traditional Owners at Starcke and the Queensland Department of Agriculture, Fisheries and Forestry, which they are using to develop a national feral pig policy and legislative framework.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
